{
  "concept": "When the lower boundary of a plane region is given by two different formulas on either side of x = 0, the region must be split into two type-I sub-regions and the double integral written as a sum of two iterated integrals.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrow and label 'x', tick marks and labels at -2, 0, 2",
    "Vertical y-axis with arrow and label 'y' (origin at intersection, y-axis drawn tall enough to reach above y = 3)",
    "Triangular region with vertices (-2, 3), (0, 1), (2, 3) outlined in thick blue",
    "Top boundary segment y = 3 from (-2,3) to (2,3), thick blue, with label 'y = 3' placed twice (above the left half and above the right half)",
    "Lower-left boundary segment y = -x + 1 from (-2,3) to (0,1), thick blue, labeled 'y = -x + 1' to the left of the region",
    "Lower-right boundary segment y = x + 1 from (0,1) to (2,3), thick blue, labeled 'y = x + 1' to the right of the region",
    "Left sub-region R1 (x from -2 to 0) shaded pale blue with interior label 'R₁'",
    "Right sub-region R2 (x from 0 to 2) shaded pale orange/yellow with interior label 'R₂'",
    "Red vertical strip inside R1 at about x = -1, running from the lower boundary up to y = 3",
    "Red vertical strip inside R2 at about x = 0.7, running from the lower boundary up to y = 3",
    "Dashed vertical divider along the y-axis marking the split between R1 and R2",
    "Iterated-integral readout panel showing ∫₋₂⁰∫_{-x+1}^{3} f dy dx + ∫₀²∫_{x+1}^{3} f dy dx",
    "Small caption box showing the current lower-boundary formulas for each side"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "lowerBoundaries",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "label": "Lower boundary of each sub-region",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "leftLower",
          "label": "R₁ lower: y =",
          "default": "-x + 1",
          "variables": ["x"],
          "domain": [-2, 0],
          "role": "boundary"
        },
        {
          "id": "rightLower",
          "label": "R₂ lower: y =",
          "default": "x + 1",
          "variables": ["x"],
          "domain": [0, 2],
          "role": "boundary"
        }
      ],
      "teaches": "The inner integral's lower limit IS the lower boundary formula; because that formula changes expression at x = 0, one single iterated integral cannot describe the whole region."
    },
    {
      "id": "stripX",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Vertical strip position x",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.05],
      "default": -1,
      "teaches": "Sweeping the red strip across the region shows the inner integral running from y = lower(x) up to y = 3, and shows the lower endpoint jumping formulas as the strip crosses x = 0.",
      "...": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Region view",
      "options": ["Whole region R", "Split into R₁ and R₂"],
      "default": "Split into R₁ and R₂",
      "teaches": "Seeing R as one shape versus two colored halves makes clear that the split is a bookkeeping device for the limits, not a change to the region itself."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The region R",
      "narration": "R is the triangle bounded above by y = 3 and below by the two lines y = -x + 1 and y = x + 1, with vertices (-2, 3), (0, 1) and (2, 3).",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Whole region R", "stripX": -1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A vertical strip on the left",
      "narration": "Fix x between -2 and 0. The red strip runs from the lower boundary y = -x + 1 up to y = 3, so the inner integral is ∫ from -x+1 to 3 dy.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Split into R₁ and R₂", "stripX": -1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Crossing x = 0",
      "narration": "Drag the strip past the y-axis: the top of the strip stays at y = 3, but the bottom suddenly follows y = x + 1 instead. The lower limit changes formula exactly at x = 0.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Split into R₁ and R₂", "stripX": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two integrals, one region",
      "narration": "Because no single formula gives the bottom of every strip, R is written as R₁ ∪ R₂ and the double integral becomes ∫₋₂⁰∫_{-x+1}^{3} + ∫₀²∫_{x+1}^{3}.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Split into R₁ and R₂", "stripX": 1.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Edit a boundary",
      "narration": "Type a new lower boundary for either half — say 'x^2 + 1' on the right. The blue curve, the shaded area, and the corresponding inner limit all update together, confirming that the limit is the boundary.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Split into R₁ and R₂", "stripX": 1.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The inner limits of a type-I double integral are literally the upper and lower boundary curves; when the lower boundary switches formula at x = 0, you must switch integrals there too.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Centered coordinate axes in black with arrowheads, x-axis labeled at -2, 0, 2 and a tall y-axis. A downward-pointing triangle sits above the x-axis with vertices (-2,3), (0,1), (2,3), outlined in a thick medium-blue stroke. Left half (x<0) filled pale blue labeled R₁; right half filled pale peach/yellow labeled R₂; the y-axis acts as the divider. Labels 'y = 3' sit just above the top edge on both halves, 'y = -x + 1' outside the region to the left, 'y = x + 1' outside to the right, all in serif italic math style. Two thin red vertical segments inside the halves run from the slanted lower edge up to the top edge, representing inner integration in y. Keep generous whitespace; the whole triangle occupies the upper-middle of the canvas with the x-axis low. Add a small readout of the iterated integral sum below or beside the figure, updating live with the edited boundary expressions."
}

